            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "kstuff"
            },
            "reviewBody": "If light corn syrup is not available, you can substitute a sugar syrup made with 1-1/4 cups sugar and 1/3 cup water, boiled together until syrupy."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "RUBINI"
            },
            "reviewBody": "It's easy and makes for a nice professional shiny and smooth finish. It's a very sweet icing but aren't they all? I did have to add a bit more milk and corn syrup to make it into a spreadable consistancy. Make sure you don't add too much of either ... otherwise it gets too runny. I used the almond extract as suggested but have also substituted it with clear vanilla and lemon extract. I think any extract will work. It takes a few of hours to harden and is stackable after that. I used a thin layer of this icing with the Best Rolled Sugar Cookies Recipe. This made a thick professional looking cookie.",
            "datePublished": "2002-09-11T07:26:13.857Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Hungrymama"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Used 5 teaspoons milk, 1 teaspoon vanilla extract, and 1 Tablespoon light corn syrup. Added drops of food coloring, swirled with a toothpick and then dipped the cookies to get the the dye look. Very fun! Can’t wait to eat!!!",
            "datePublished": "2020-12-28T22:11:05.263Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "cooking_fanatic87"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Fantastic! I followed the other comments with 1 cup powdered sugar, 4 tsp milk, 1 tsp almond extract, and 1 tbsp corn syrup. I used this for piping and then thinned down the remaining frosting with another tsp of milk and used it for flooding. My first time ever frosting cookies this way and I loved how they turned out!",
            "datePublished": "2019-10-13T17:34:23.180Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Noelle Barto"
            },
            "reviewBody": "First time using icing instead of frosting for my sugar cookies, did have to add more milk to get powder sugar \"smooth\". Got to perfect consistency to use pastry brush, which is much quicker than frosting with a knife. Definitely my new go to for cut-out cookies.",
            "datePublished": "2020-05-15T04:30:15.003Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
